<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  DEBUG BUILDS ONLY. This file lives in src/debug, so the manifest merger applies
  it to debug builds and never to release ones.

  Why it exists: Android has blocked cleartext (plain HTTP) traffic by default
  since API 28, but the .NET backend runs over plain HTTP on a developer's machine
  during local development. Without this, requests fail on the device before they
  ever reach the network.

  Why it is permissive: cleartext is allowed to ANY host rather than to a
  whitelist of specific IPs. An earlier version pinned each developer's LAN IP,
  which meant every new contributor had to edit this tracked file with their own
  address — a merge-conflict magnet and a silent failure when forgotten. Since the
  exception cannot reach a release build, the practical risk is limited to
  developer machines on their own networks.

  Do NOT add android:usesCleartextTraffic to the manifest as well: Android ignores
  that attribute whenever a networkSecurityConfig is present, so the two do not
  combine and having both is misleading.

  For production: the backend should be served over HTTPS with a CA-issued
  certificate, at which point nothing here is needed. If a staging environment
  ever uses a private CA, add a <domain-config> with <trust-anchors> for that host
  rather than loosening this further.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
